  >
    <h2>Context</h2>
    <p>
      As part of my <strong>Networks & Telecommunications</strong> degree, I wanted to push
      self-hosting to its maximum: <strong>hosting my own AI assistant</strong>, without depending
      on OpenAI, Anthropic or any paid cloud service.
    </p>
    <p>The goal was threefold:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Full autonomy</strong>: no external API calls, everything runs on my machine
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Absolute confidentiality</strong>: conversations never leave my infrastructure
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Accessibility</strong>: be able to chat with the assistant from apps I use daily
        (Telegram and WhatsApp)
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This project allowed me to combine <strong>three domains</strong> I particularly enjoy:{' '}
      <strong>Linux system administration</strong>, <strong>virtualization</strong>, and{' '}
      <strong>service integration via APIs</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <p>The entire system relies on a simple but robust flow:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>A user</strong> sends a message from Telegram or WhatsApp
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The OpenClaw gateway</strong> (Node.js process) receives the request and identifies
        the source channel
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It transmits it to Ollama</strong>, which runs the <code>phi3:mini</code> model
        locally on CPU
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The response</strong> is sent back to the user via the same channel
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Everything is hosted on a <strong>VM Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS</strong> virtualized under VMware
      Workstation Pro. Ports <code>18789</code> (gateway) and <code>11434</code> (Ollama) are{' '}
      <strong>never publicly exposed</strong>: dashboard access goes through an{' '}
      <strong>encrypted SSH tunnel</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Implementation steps</h2>

    <h3>1. VM preparation</h3>
    <p>
      Deliberately generous on CPU (24 cores) but modest on RAM (6 GB), since the{' '}
      <code>phi3:mini</code> model fits comfortably in 3 GB once loaded.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Stack installation</h3>
    <p>Two major components:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Node.js 22.x</strong>: required to run OpenClaw
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>OpenClaw</strong>: CLI installed globally via npm
      </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_22.x | sudo -E bash -
sudo apt install -y nodejs
sudo npm install -g openclaw@latest</code></pre>

    <h3>3. Ollama + phi3:mini</h3>
    <p>The model choice was made after several tests:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Model</th>
          <th>Size</th>
          <th>CPU Speed</th>
          <th>RAM</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <strong>Mistral 7B</strong>
          </td>
          <td>4.1 GB</td>
          <td>11 tokens/s</td>
          <td>5 GB</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <strong>phi3:mini</strong>
          </td>
          <td>2.2 GB</td>
          <td>
            <strong>24 tokens/s</strong>
          </td>
          <td>2.5 GB</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>phi3:mini</code> offers the <strong>best speed/memory compromise</strong> for a GPU-less
      environment. <code>OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE</code> was configured to 10 minutes to avoid model
      reloading between two requests.
    </p>

    <h3>4. OpenClaw gateway configuration</h3>
    <p>
      The gateway was configured in <strong>strict local mode</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>No access from outside</li>
      <li>Authentication disabled (restricted by SSH tunnel)</li>
      <li>User whitelist for Telegram</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Telegram integration</h3>
    <p>
      Creation of the <code>@flavien_ai_bot</code> bot via <strong>BotFather</strong>, then token
      and whitelist configuration:
    </p>
    <pre><code>openclaw config set channels.telegram.botToken "TOKEN"
openclaw config set channels.telegram.enabled true
openclaw pairing approve telegram RDP2NSTB</code></pre>
    <p>
      The <strong>pairing</strong> mechanism is a real security plus: even if the bot token leaked,
      an unknown user would have to be manually approved.
    </p>

    <h3>6. WhatsApp integration</h3>
    <p>
      Trickier. WhatsApp doesn't offer a free official API for a personal account, so I used a{' '}
      <strong>community plugin</strong> (via ClawHub) that bridges with WhatsApp Web:
    </p>
    <pre><code>openclaw channels add
# Select WhatsApp (QR link)
# Scan the QR code from the phone
openclaw config set plugins.allow '["whatsapp"]'</code></pre>
    <p>
      The plugin maintains a persistent session, allowing messages to be received without rescanning
      on each restart.
    </p>

    <h2>Challenges encountered</h2>
    <p>This project was a real learning ground — many things don't go as planned the first time.</p>

    <h3>🔐 No GPU access from VM</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: my RTX 4060 was not accessible in VMware (GPU virtualization
      limitation on Windows).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: I had to abandon heavy models and choose a{' '}
      <strong>CPU-optimized model</strong>. <code>phi3:mini</code> was the best candidate.
    </p>

    <h3>📦 npm permission error</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: <code>npm install -g openclaw</code> returned{' '}
      <code>EACCES: permission denied</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: <code>sudo npm install -g</code> — not elegant but fast.
    </p>

    <h3>🔑 Dashboard authentication broken</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: <code>token_missing</code> loop, unable to connect to{' '}
      <code>127.0.0.1:18789</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: switch to <code>gateway.auth.mode none</code>{' '}
      <strong>combined with an SSH tunnel</strong>.
    </p>

    <h3>🐢 Initial model slowness</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: Mistral 7B ran at <strong>11 tokens/s</strong> on CPU.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: migration to <code>phi3:mini</code> which reaches{' '}
      <strong>24 tokens/s</strong>, i.e. an average response time of <strong>2-3 seconds</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Result</h2>
    <p>
      The assistant has been <strong>operational 24/7</strong> for several weeks:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        ✅ Accessible via <strong>Telegram</strong> (
        <a href="https://t.me/flavien_ai_bot">@flavien_ai_bot</a>)
      </li>
      <li>
        ✅ Accessible via <strong>WhatsApp</strong> (personal account)
      </li>
      <li>
        ✅ Local dashboard on <code>127.0.0.1:18789</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        ✅ Responds in <strong>French</strong> (personalization via <code>SOUL.md</code>)
      </li>
      <li>✅ No data sent to third parties</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Skills demonstrated</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Linux administration</strong>: systemd service management, bash scripts, permissions
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Virtualization</strong>: VMware configuration, resource allocation
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI / LLM</strong>: local deployment, model selection, CPU optimization
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>API integration</strong>: Telegram Bot API, WhatsApp Web, webhooks
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Network security</strong>: SSH tunnel, isolation, token management
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Improvement paths</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Adding custom skills</strong>: weather, reminders, tech watch
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Migration to a more powerful model</strong>: <code>qwen2.5:7b</code> if RAM allows
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Monitoring</strong>: performance dashboard, crash alerts
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Continuous deployment</strong>: GitHub Actions pipeline for OpenClaw updates
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I take away</h2>
    <p>
      This project taught me <strong>much more</strong> than I imagined:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Constraint drives inventiveness</strong> — no GPU? Find the model that works on CPU.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Security is thought in layers</strong> — SSH tunnel + whitelist + network isolation
        = defense in depth.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Self-hosting means choosing your dependencies</strong> — zero dependency on major
        cloud APIs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Observability matters</strong> — logs, dashboard, tokens/s: impossible to optimize
        what you don't measure.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      This is typically the kind of project where{' '}
      <strong>the journey is as important as the destination</strong>.
    </p>
  </ProjectLayout>
</BaseLayout>
